  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height as a surface with contours; it is 3 times steeper across x, so overshoot shows there first. Without noise, x steps grow when learning rate > 2/3; at 1 the run diverges in a few steps.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per component has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 3× their true length, one shared scale. Purple: this mini-batch step (where the ball goes next). Dark: the full-data step, against the true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the ball leaves the drawn bowl or the loss exceeds 6. Off-bowl points are drawn at the rim; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (up to 41); the kept ghost path likewise. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence; each new run uses the next noise values."
  ]
}